# Header

The shell's top bar: page title, global patient search, the
notification drawer and the user menu.

<%= embed :header, :default %>

## When to use

- Once, in the application layout — every page gets it. Pages
  configure it through its locals, never re-render their own.

## When not to use

- Page-specific actions or filters — those live in the page content
  beside what they act on (see the *Navigation* pattern page: the
  header is global, section-independent tooling).
- Secondary toolbars — compose page-level rows from buttons; don't
  imitate header chrome.

## Usage

`render_header` (`Gesso::Components::HeaderHelper`) computes the unread
notification count and renders the bar. Call it once, in the application
layout.

```erb
<%%= render_header(
  title: content_for(:title),
  user_name: "Jane Smith",
  user_initials: "JS",
  user_email: "jane.smith@nhs.net",
  notifications: @notifications
) %>
```

| Param | Default | Notes |
| ----- | ------- | ----- |
| `title` | nil | the page `<h1>` |
| `show_search` | `true` | global search field |
| `user_name` / `user_initials` / `user_email` | — | user menu identity |
| `notifications` | `[]` | drawer items; unread count badges the bell (capped "9+") |

The notification drawer is the `sheet` partial. The header is
re-rendered on each Turbo navigation and its basecoat controls (the
user menu, the drawer) are re-initialised by the `turbo:load` handler —
it must not be `data-turbo-permanent`, or the re-init stacks a
duplicate dropdown listener that cancels the next click.

## Do and don't

- Do pass the page title through `title:` — one `<h1>` per page,
  and the header is where it renders.
- Do cap what enters `notifications` at what the drawer can
  usefully show; it is a recency surface, not an archive.
- Don't hide the search (`show_search: false`) without a reason —
  global patient lookup is the header's main job.
- Don't add new global controls casually; every header item taxes
  every page.

## Accessibility and content

- The bell carries the unread count in text (not just a dot), and
  the drawer is a labelled sheet with native dismissal.
- Title is the page name as the sidebar knows it — shell and
  content must agree on what this page is called.
